# mix-of-capabilities 04×09×21 — la risposta derivata dice la catena e le fonti delle
# premesse. «does rain make surfaces slippery?» → «Yes: …» → «how do you know that?»
# riceveva «That sounds nice». Ora dice che la conclusione e' propria e cita, premessa
# per premessa, la frase data (fact_source) o la base curata
# (kb/core/causal-questions.p0).
[mock live]
!set PARROT0_SESSION=
!set PARROT0_PROFILE=kb/profiles/agi.p0
!set PARROT0_LANG=en
!reset

[test a derived chain names its premises from the curated base]
!timeout 30
> does rain make surfaces slippery?
<~ Yes: rain causes wet ground
> how do you know that?
<~ I derived it from what I hold
<~ wet ground causes slippery surfaces (part of my curated base)

[test a premise given as a sentence is quoted]
!timeout 30
> slippery surfaces cause falls
<~ Learned
> does rain lead to falls?
<~ Yes: rain causes wet ground
> where does that come from?
<~ wet ground causes slippery surfaces (part of my curated base)
<~ slippery surfaces causes falls (from the sentence «slippery surfaces cause falls»)

[test a different question in between retires the derivation]
!timeout 30
> does rain make surfaces slippery?
> can I mix bleach and ammonia?
> how do you know that?
<! I derived it

[test a link read from an attenuated sentence is attenuated in the chain]
!reset
!timeout 30
> do poorly maintained cooling towers cause legionnaires disease?
<~ poorly maintained cooling towers causes legionella outbreaks (in most cases), and legionella outbreaks causes legionnaires disease.
> how do you know that?
<~ (in most cases) (from the sentence «most legionella outbreaks result from poorly maintained cooling towers»)
